{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"fc4c2639-1d2f-40ac-a4be-b6c4d8e1d0f6","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"The process termination after `run_command` completion is unexpected; need to understand why. Reviewing the tool's behavior, it's designed to execute commands within new sessions, implying working directory and environment variables are session-specific. Commands exceeding the wait time become background tasks, with notifications. Need to investigate why this session-specific execution is leading to termination.\n\n\n"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"fc4c2639-1d2f-40ac-a4be-b6c4d8e1d0f6","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"The Windows environment employs Job Objects that terminate child processes when the parent session ends, specifically due to `JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`. This explains why processes started by `run_command` were killed immediately after the session ended, even if intended to run continuously. The solution lies in using the built-in `IsDaemon: true` functionality of `run_command`, which is designed for long-running processes like development servers that should persist in the background.\n\n\n"}}}}
